00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:08.950
OK.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:14.692
So what is Cloud Speech API, and
why are we so excited about it?

00:06:14.692 --> 00:06:20.706
So it's our neural network
based speech recognition.

00:06:20.706 --> 00:06:22.180
I already mentioned before--

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:24.820
we're making it available
for the first time now.

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:30.430
And it's universally available
across 80-plus languages

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:31.030
and variants.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:41.250
Basically, I think more
than half of the people

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:45.120
that use smartphones
today around the world

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.450
are already covered, and we're
aiming for more than that.

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:53.350
You can use it in
two different modes--

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.460
real time streaming and batch.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:59.590
And I'm going to show you a
little bit of both later today,

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:01.985
and it's accurate in
noisy environments

00:07:01.985 --> 00:07:03.110
and with different accents.

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:06.650
So when we built our
speech recognition,

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:10.730
we needed it to work for
people that are walking around

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.310
in Times Square, walking
around with their phones,

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:16.160
and asking their phone,
what's the weather like in New

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.520
York, while there's 100 other
people right next to them.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.310
And so it knows how to
filter background voices.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.040
But there's also people in India
that are walking the street,

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.920
and there's a bus passing
right next to them.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:31.280
And so it knows how to
filter all different kinds

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.690
of background noises and
focus on the primary speaker.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:41.400
And the last thing here
I wanted to emphasize--

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:44.400
we have a really easy
way of customizing speech

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.780
to your domain.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:49.470
So if you have a name of a
company or a name of a product

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.900
that's not actually a word
in English or in the language

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:55.840
that you're trying to work
in, we make it really easy.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:58.230
All you need to do is just
tell us what that word is,

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.480
and we automatically add it
to the dictionary for you,

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.220
and we can start recognizing it.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.470
If you're in a specific
mode in your application

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:08.190
where you're expecting a certain
command-- so if you're building

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:12.540
speech recognition for a player,
and you're playing a movie,

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.460
so you might want to
detect stop and pause.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.168
But then when you're
not playing a movie,

00:08:16.168 --> 00:08:18.880
you might want to detect
play or other words.

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:20.940
You can use word
and phrase hints

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:25.200
to detect exactly the words
you need in the right context.

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:34.190
So we have three different
methods for using Speech API--

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:36.799
streaming, sync, and async.

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:42.539
Streaming allows you to
process speech in real time

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:46.940
while the user is saying
it, and so it's the fastest

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.710
way of using Speech API.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:53.150
It works with
something called gRPC,

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:58.412
which is our improved
binary version of REST.

00:08:58.412 --> 00:09:00.620
The gRPC people are going
to kill me for saying that,

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:05.990
but it's a faster, more
efficient protocol and way

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:10.220
of sort of accessing
remote procedures,

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:13.730
and it allows you to
process any audio,

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:18.200
up to a minute, that's included
inline in the requests.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:23.940
Sync is a much
easier-to-use method.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:26.540
You can use it on REST API.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:29.420
You can even do it
with a cURL command,

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:32.630
and it can process any
audio, up to a minute.

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:35.000
You can either embed
the audio inline

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.170
or use Google Cloud Storage.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.700
And then we have
async, which allows

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.980
you to process larger files.

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:46.350
It's basically not limited.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:49.690
It says up to 80
minutes, but that

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:53.470
can be increased if you
get a quota increase.

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:57.730
And it can also be
accessed by REST

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.470
and can process
files either inline

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:01.840
or at Google Cloud Storage.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.320
So with async, because
it's not blocking,

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:09.520
you can quickly process
a large number of files

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.247
and then just check back in to
see if they're done processing.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:23.860
In terms of standards, when I
wrote this slide I was even--

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:27.130
I forgot how many different
standards we support.

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:28.920
It's pretty universal.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:31.910
That's something
we're very proud of.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:34.204
There's a large list of
programming languages

00:10:34.204 --> 00:10:35.620
you can use to
access Cloud Speech

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:40.570
API and a large list of audio
encodings that we support.

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:46.030
Now, this is not an experimental
project that someone at Google

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:46.780
is running.

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:50.980
This is a commercial
service that we're fully

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.880
backing and really developing,
investing a lot in.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:01.930
So I wanted to give you
guys just a little bit

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:04.030
of best practices.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:06.970
If you are developing
speech recognition apps,

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:08.980
what would we recommend?

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:13.180
And our first slide is about
human-computer interaction,

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:14.770
so the left-hand
side of the use cases

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.390
that we spoke about earlier.

00:11:17.390 --> 00:11:19.480
So for human-computer
interaction,

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.540
you really want to use
streaming whenever you can.

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:24.809
When you can't use
streaming, you can use sync.

00:11:24.809 --> 00:11:26.350
But then there's
going to be a delay,

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:29.150
and users are waiting
for a response,

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:31.210
so it's not recommended.

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:34.640
You always want to use
uncompressed audio if you can.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.610
And if you want to
compress, you should

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:40.870
use lossless compression,
because lossy compression,

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:44.470
like mp3s or things
like that, they really

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:48.480
degrade the accuracy
of the recognition.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:54.430
Ideally, you want to use as high
of a sampling rate as you can,

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:59.200
but at least above 16 kilohertz,
or 16 kilohertz or more.

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:01.630
You don't want to
go below that if you

00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:06.780
want to use human-computer
interaction,

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:11.080
if you have a choice, if you're
not running on phone lines.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:13.760
And whenever you can, you
want to use speech context

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:18.220
to get the best-accuracy
results that you can.

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:24.880
Now, using Speech API
requires that you specify

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:26.170
the language in advance.

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:29.020
So what we see a lot
of app developers do

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.420
is they detect the
language of the OS.

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:32.920
And that way they
know what language

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:35.687
the user is going to speak,
and they provide that

00:12:35.687 --> 00:12:36.520
to Cloud Speech API.

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:43.010
So then there's the
other set of use cases,

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:44.630
which are speech analytics.

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:47.240
So there you're
typically dealing

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:51.590
with longer-form
audio-- like, a call

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:54.080
could be 5 minutes, 10
minutes, 15 minutes.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:58.520
A movie could be one hour,
two hours, three hours.

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:01.580
So you want to use
async, most probably.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:04.550
You want to store those
files on Google Cloud Storage

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:08.030
and then process them in batch.

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:10.830
And if there's
multiple speakers,

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:15.980
you may want to separate the
streams so that you can process

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:19.570
the speakers separately.

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:22.030
If you're processing
a video, you

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:25.960
might want to use a tool like
FFmpeg to strip out the audio

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:30.520
and just send the audio
to Cloud Speech API.

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:34.060
And it's very easy to combine
it with Natural Language API

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:35.770
and Translation
API, which you're

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:37.150
going to see in a second.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:41.550
OK.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:45.630
I wanted to give you
guys a few examples

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:48.930
of human-computer
interaction, because we're

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:51.930
going to spend more time
later about speech analytics.

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:59.130
So Azar is a video-chat
discovery app.

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:03.400
Does anyone here in this
room know about Azar?

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:12.300
OK, a few-- so apparently
they have 15 billion discovery

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:13.050
matches already.

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:16.320
So what they do is they
connect people with new people

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:19.200
that they don't
know, and they've

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:21.490
done it 15 billion
times already.

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:26.140
Yeah, every time I hear
about this, I'm just shocked.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:29.244
And most of the
time or many times,

00:14:29.244 --> 00:14:30.660
it's people in
different countries

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:32.650
that speak different languages.

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:35.877
And there's a little bit
of a language barrier,

00:14:35.877 --> 00:14:38.460
like you can imagine, when two
people from different countries

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:40.240
want to talk to each other.

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:45.030
So what they do is they use
Cloud Speech API to transcribe

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:47.130
the speech into
text, and then they

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:51.210
use Translation API to
convert that from one language

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:53.760
to another, and they allow two
people in different countries

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:54.676
to talk to each other.

00:14:54.676 --> 00:14:57.750
And as they're talking,
they see on the screen

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:00.250
what the other person is saying,
and they can react to that.

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:01.083
So it's pretty cool.

00:15:01.083 --> 00:15:05.370
It's really sort of enabling
use cases that were not really

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:09.090
possible even one
or two years ago--

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:10.020
very, very exciting.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:17.310
Another example is Tribe,
which is a messaging

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:18.870
app that uses video.

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:22.470
And it allows people that
don't want to type a message--

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:25.320
they just want to say
something to their friends.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:28.290
They can record a quick video,
say whatever they want to say,

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:31.190
and Tribe will show
the video [AUDIO OUT]

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:35.010
enable adding text to that
video by transcribing the text

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:37.289
and defining some
sort of keywords,

00:15:37.289 --> 00:15:38.580
or alerts, or things like that.

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:41.940
So if you mention a dinner,
or a restaurant, or something,

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:43.980
it can react to that
and give the user

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:47.830
more information about what
they're talking about--

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:48.330
pretty cool.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:51.960
OK.

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:57.090
So now I want to show
you guys a few demos.

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:59.560
Can we switch to
the demo computer?

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:00.640
Yeah.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:02.970
Cool.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.700
So this is always risky.

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:09.340
Speech recognition demos
in a large room with echo

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:12.240
is not what it was intended for.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.310
But what I'm going
to show you now--

00:16:14.310 --> 00:16:17.070
I'm just using a
regular computer,

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:20.310
and I'm using the mic from the
computer-- from my computer--

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:22.470
nothing special, no
special equipment.

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:24.540
I'm just going to talk
close to the computer,

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:27.900
and let's see what
it's able to do.

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:46.390
I think it got 100%.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:48.640
I'm not sure.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:51.024
Usually it gets
100% or close to it.

00:16:51.024 --> 00:16:52.690
It doesn't always get
every single word,

00:16:52.690 --> 00:16:55.980
but I think this
time it might have.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:58.054
And I don't know if
you guys noticed,

00:16:58.054 --> 00:16:59.470
but while I was
talking, you could

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:02.890
see on the top the words
were appearing in real time

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:06.250
almost in the exact same
moment that I said the word.

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:08.200
So it's pretty impressive.

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:11.240
It's very, very fast.

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:14.980
The other thing I would
say is interesting

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:19.420
here is you can see that I
said the word meet twice.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:22.540
And even though I pronounced
it in exactly the same way,

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:23.859
it knows how to differentiate.

00:17:23.859 --> 00:17:25.450
And from the context
of the sentence,

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:28.870
it knows when I said
you know this meet

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:31.000
and when I said that meat.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:35.500
So every time this
works, I'm always amazed.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:37.330
It's pretty cool.

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:41.130
Let me show you something else.

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:45.887
I really love Taylor Swift,
which is true, by the way.

00:17:45.887 --> 00:17:48.400
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:48.900
OK.

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:51.190
We have a bug in the demo here.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:58.500
But you can see this demo uses
a couple different elements.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:02.250
So one is it combines Speech
API with Natural Language API.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:05.280
So you can see the overall
sentiment is positive,

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:06.720
so it detected that.

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:10.890
And it also extracted
the entity, Taylor Swift.

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:12.545
And it brought up a card.

00:18:12.545 --> 00:18:14.904
It knows it's a
singer-songwriter.

00:18:14.904 --> 00:18:17.070
It has a little bit of a
description from Wikipedia,

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:18.870
and you can click on the link.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:20.370
But the other thing
I wanted to show

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:26.280
you is our speech recognition
was built originally

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:28.750
for our search engine.

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:32.920
And so it's built to recognize
named entities really,

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:34.350
really well.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:37.972
We get searches for every
single entity you could imagine,

00:18:37.972 --> 00:18:39.930
and a lot of those searches
are voice searches.

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:42.810
And so we developed our
speech recognition in a way

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:44.880
that it knows how
to handle that.

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:49.830
And when you mention
entities with speech,

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:52.770
we will detect them, even if
it's not a word in English,

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:54.070
and we will format them.

00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:56.430
So you can see it's
capitalized, and it knows

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:59.830
how to recognize the entity.

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:03.150
So the third and last demo--

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:06.960
so this is a demo
that sort of tries

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:11.690
to teach you how to speak a
new language with Speech API

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:13.650
and with Translation API.

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:19.470
So it's going to detect
that I'm in an airport

00:19:19.470 --> 00:19:21.510
and about to fly to Tokyo.

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:24.502
And it's going to try
and teach me Japanese.

00:19:24.502 --> 00:19:25.710
COMPUTER: [SPEAKING JAPANESE]

00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:27.632
DAN AHARON: Did
you guys hear that?

00:19:27.632 --> 00:19:28.840
COMPUTER: [SPEAKING JAPANESE]

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:29.480
DAN AHARON: OK.

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:31.530
It wants me to say [JAPANESE].

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:37.170
So I'll try and get this wrong,
and let's see what happens.

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:38.665
[SPEAKING JAPANESE]

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:47.190
OK, that wasn't wrong enough.

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:48.124
Let me--

00:19:48.124 --> 00:19:49.992
COMPUTER: [SPEAKING JAPANESE]

00:19:49.992 --> 00:19:51.830
DAN AHARON: Let me try this one.

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:58.073
[SPEAKING JAPANESE]

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:07.370
So apparently, what I said in
Japanese is, "King [JAPANESE]

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:09.320
soccer."

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:12.920
So it actually recognized
my language in Japanese

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:17.480
and then translated
it back into English--

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.900
so pretty cool.

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:20.960
OK.

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:23.990
So these are pre-prepared demos.

00:20:23.990 --> 00:20:27.980
And you'll say, OK, that's
fine, but how easy is it really

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:28.650
