[LAUGHTER]

00:33:04.185 --> 00:33:05.004
OK.

00:33:05.004 --> 00:33:06.670
If we can go back to
the slides, please.

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:14.300
We did have some
technical challenges

00:33:14.300 --> 00:33:17.780
when implementing Cloud
Speech API, the first of which

00:33:17.780 --> 00:33:19.400
was time stamps.

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:20.990
And I don't know
if any of you here

00:33:20.990 --> 00:33:23.150
are a part of the user
groups, but that's

00:33:23.150 --> 00:33:26.600
a topic that came
up quite frequently.

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:30.440
Many phone calls are 15
minutes or longer in length,

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:32.690
and an important part
of our user experience

00:33:32.690 --> 00:33:36.350
is allowing a person to
click on a keyword or phrase

00:33:36.350 --> 00:33:38.240
and skip ahead to a
phone conversation,

00:33:38.240 --> 00:33:43.160
to the point at which that
word or phrase was spoken.

00:33:43.160 --> 00:33:46.580
Currently, Cloud Speech API
does not provide time stamps.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:49.730
But we did devise a
way to implement them

00:33:49.730 --> 00:33:51.710
that works for us.

00:33:51.710 --> 00:33:54.570
The way we did this
is as follows--

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:57.230
we pre-process the
audio to detect silence

00:33:57.230 --> 00:33:59.870
in between words and phrases.

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:03.890
We then use that detected
silence to segment the file

00:34:03.890 --> 00:34:09.409
and submit those segments
individually to Cloud API.

00:34:09.409 --> 00:34:11.420
The responses we get
back we associate

00:34:11.420 --> 00:34:14.179
with the segment in the
interval at which we detected.

00:34:14.179 --> 00:34:18.380
And it allows us to know that,
for instance, Segment A, which

00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:22.070
comprised the first 8/10
of the conversation,

00:34:22.070 --> 00:34:23.960
was the word Google.

00:34:23.960 --> 00:34:27.050
We repeat that process
for each word and phrase

00:34:27.050 --> 00:34:28.670
in the recording,
and it enables us

00:34:28.670 --> 00:34:31.550
to construct a transcription
of exactly what happened

00:34:31.550 --> 00:34:34.400
on the call and the time
in which it occurred.

00:34:37.190 --> 00:34:40.270
Another issue we ran into
was speaker diarization.

00:34:40.270 --> 00:34:44.469
As we're a telephony
company, we have

00:34:44.469 --> 00:34:46.320
two sides of the conversation.

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:48.850
Cloud Speech wasn't really
designed, initially,

00:34:48.850 --> 00:34:50.590
for this type of conversation.

00:34:50.590 --> 00:34:54.190
So we record our
files in stereo,

00:34:54.190 --> 00:34:56.590
which gives us a
caller on one channel

00:34:56.590 --> 00:34:58.970
and the callee on the other.

00:34:58.970 --> 00:35:01.120
Now, if we were to
take that audio file

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:02.860
and separate them
into two channels,

00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:06.220
we'd end up with
one minute of audio,

00:35:06.220 --> 00:35:08.865
one minute of a phone call,
and two minutes of audio-- one

00:35:08.865 --> 00:35:10.990
minute for the left side,
one minute for the right,

00:35:10.990 --> 00:35:16.660
effectively doubling our cost
for processing a stereo file.

00:35:16.660 --> 00:35:19.240
In order to mitigate
that we expanded

00:35:19.240 --> 00:35:23.500
our pre-processing algorithm to
detect large spans of silence.

00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:26.230
We then remove that
silence from the audio

00:35:26.230 --> 00:35:29.110
and submit the
segments individually,

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:30.580
keeping track of
whether that was

00:35:30.580 --> 00:35:32.455
submitted for the left
side or the right side

00:35:32.455 --> 00:35:34.860
of the conversation.

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:37.920
We then reconstruct the
transcription, using the method

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:40.230
that I showed you earlier,
associating segments

00:35:40.230 --> 00:35:42.960
with time intervals, but
also associating them

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:44.810
with the caller or callee.

00:35:50.802 --> 00:35:55.670
Our experience with Cloud Speech
API has been pretty awesome.

00:35:55.670 --> 00:35:59.500
I learned a term yesterday,
which is dark data.

00:35:59.500 --> 00:36:02.790
And for us, recordings-- and
for probably a lot of you

00:36:02.790 --> 00:36:06.860
that may have audio that happens
today in recording format,

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:07.700
it's just dark data.

00:36:07.700 --> 00:36:10.470
You don't know what's
going on there.

00:36:10.470 --> 00:36:12.560
Well, using Cloud
Speech API, we were

00:36:12.560 --> 00:36:14.810
able to shed light
on our recordings,

00:36:14.810 --> 00:36:18.320
and I'd like to thank you
for allowing me to share

00:36:18.320 --> 00:36:19.630
that experience with you.

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:22.920
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:24.800 --> 00:36:27.650
DAN AHARON: Maybe if you
can stay on stage, Gary,

00:36:27.650 --> 00:36:30.000
I actually have a
question for you.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:31.520
GARY GRAVES: Sure.

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:34.220
DAN AHARON: So could you
talk a little bit about--

00:36:34.220 --> 00:36:36.472
so once you transcribe
these phone calls--

00:36:36.472 --> 00:36:38.180
like, for example,
that scenario that you

00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:39.860
showed in the video,
where a customer is

00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:43.120
calling about a car
that's not in stock.

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:45.110
What do you guys do in
terms of the analytics,

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:46.190
in terms of the workflow?

00:36:46.190 --> 00:36:48.860
How do you provide value
to car dealerships?

00:36:48.860 --> 00:36:50.920
GARY GRAVES: That's
a great question.

00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:55.100
So an example is you see a car
on a website, and you call in.

00:36:55.100 --> 00:36:58.550
And for whatever reason,
that car is not available.

00:36:58.550 --> 00:37:01.040
This particular dealership
is using our system,

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:04.400
so we know exactly what's
happening on the call.

00:37:04.400 --> 00:37:06.824
In the automotive space, they
have a notion of everybody

00:37:06.824 --> 00:37:09.240
that calls in, you want to get
their contact information--

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:11.630
their name, their number,
product of interest.

00:37:11.630 --> 00:37:13.850
So that if that product
does become available,

00:37:13.850 --> 00:37:15.780
because it's a
very fluid market,

00:37:15.780 --> 00:37:19.610
you'd have a point of contact
to reach back out to them.

00:37:19.610 --> 00:37:21.930
So we identify a
couple of key factors.

00:37:21.930 --> 00:37:25.220
Number one, the
call was for sales.

00:37:25.220 --> 00:37:28.300
Number two, there was a product
that they were interested in.

00:37:28.300 --> 00:37:30.860
And number three, there
was no contact information

00:37:30.860 --> 00:37:32.370
that was collected.

00:37:32.370 --> 00:37:35.300
In our space we call that
a missed opportunity, which

00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:37.250
becomes actionable
because we'll send that

00:37:37.250 --> 00:37:39.410
to a sales manager in the
form of a text message

00:37:39.410 --> 00:37:41.630
and/or an email,
based on his settings,

00:37:41.630 --> 00:37:44.150
to let him know that this
particular opportunity may

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:45.830
have been missed.

00:37:45.830 --> 00:37:49.010
What ends up happening
in those cases--

00:37:49.010 --> 00:37:53.630
large dealerships have this
instance occurring dozens

00:37:53.630 --> 00:37:56.780
of times a week, and it really
impacts their bottom line

00:37:56.780 --> 00:37:59.330
to be able to have a
second chance at selling

00:37:59.330 --> 00:38:01.569
that particular customer.

00:38:01.569 --> 00:38:03.860
DAN AHARON: So what have been
reactions for dealerships

00:38:03.860 --> 00:38:05.372
that have tried the new system?

00:38:05.372 --> 00:38:06.830
GARY GRAVES: Well,
the reaction has

00:38:06.830 --> 00:38:08.870
been overwhelmingly positive.

00:38:08.870 --> 00:38:12.650
In fact, large dealer
groups that we've demoed

00:38:12.650 --> 00:38:17.420
and had an opportunity to show
our system to end up pushing it

00:38:17.420 --> 00:38:20.150
throughout their whole group.

00:38:20.150 --> 00:38:22.567
I don't know if I'm allowed
to mention customer names or--

00:38:22.567 --> 00:38:23.941
DAN AHARON: If
you could just say

00:38:23.941 --> 00:38:25.310
in terms of business value--

00:38:25.310 --> 00:38:26.810
are they actually
making more sales,

00:38:26.810 --> 00:38:28.509
or is this just a nice-to-have?

00:38:28.509 --> 00:38:30.800
GARY GRAVES: Well, not only
are they making more sales,

00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:35.130
but it's causing a shift in the
mentality at car dealerships,

00:38:35.130 --> 00:38:37.160
because now everyone
in the dealership

00:38:37.160 --> 00:38:39.560
is being held accountable.

00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:42.590
It's one thing to have a
recording or monitoring

00:38:42.590 --> 00:38:45.380
solution in place, and
people know it's there.

00:38:45.380 --> 00:38:47.732
But that's reactive,
meaning the only time

00:38:47.732 --> 00:38:49.190
that that information
is ever going

00:38:49.190 --> 00:38:53.120
to be leveraged is if there's
a situation that calls that

00:38:53.120 --> 00:38:54.470
into question.

00:38:54.470 --> 00:38:57.320
Whereas using Cloud
Speech we're able to mine

00:38:57.320 --> 00:39:00.320
these conversations for
actionable intelligence

00:39:00.320 --> 00:39:04.730
to allow us to empower dealers
to be proactive and provide

00:39:04.730 --> 00:39:06.380
a higher level of
customer service.

00:39:06.380 --> 00:39:09.490
And it's really becoming
disruptive in our industry.

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:10.925
DAN AHARON: Great, awesome.

00:39:10.925 --> 00:39:11.424
Thank you.

